  "response": "When a distributed database begins returning silently wrong data, the first task of a postmortem is to separate competing explanations before anyone commits to a remedy. In the incident examined here, the engineering team opened a postmortem after checksum mismatches appeared in a distributed database cluster. Two hypotheses emerged at once. The storage team argued that the corruption was caused by faulty memory hardware, whereas the database team argued that the corruption was caused by a race condition in the replication code, a position it had already staked out before the postmortem was formally opened. The disagreement is typical. Intermittent corruption is precisely the symptom that both a concurrency defect and a marginal hardware component can produce. The investigation must therefore rest on evidence that discriminates between the two mechanisms, not on which team states its case first or most forcefully.\n\nThe most discriminating evidence is usually the distribution of failures. Engineers correlate every corruption event with software version, host identity, rack position, firmware revision, and time of occurrence, looking for the variable that partitions affected nodes from healthy ones. Here the correlation with software was stark: the corruption appeared only on nodes running build 4.2.1. In other words, every node that exhibited corruption was running build 4.2.1. The correlation with physical infrastructure pointed the same way. The corruption events were spread across many different physical hosts, a dispersion that results from the fact that hardware faults typically cluster on specific machines rather than spreading evenly across a fleet. A version boundary and a fleet-wide scatter together form the signature that investigators associate with a code path rather than a component.\n\nHardware telemetry is the next line of inquiry, and it must be read with a clear understanding of what the error-correction layer guarantees. ECC memory corrects all multi-bit memory errors, and for that reason the machine-check logs on the affected hosts showed no uncorrected memory errors. Telemetry of this kind narrows the field without closing it, because a clean log describes what the hardware reported rather than everything the hardware did. By the end of the log review, the team had established that at least one of two defects was present, perhaps both: a race condition existed in the replication log apply path, or a memory module in the cluster had a defective cell. Neither possibility could yet be excluded on the strength of logs and distributions alone.\n\nDynamic analysis then supplies the decisive test. ThreadSanitizer can detect bit flips caused by faulty DRAM. By contrast, and despite the defective memory cell, when the team replayed the replication workload under instrumentation, ThreadSanitizer reported a data race in the replication log apply path, and that report confirms that the race in the apply path was real rather than merely inferred from correlations. Engineers then reproduced the mismatch deterministically by injecting scheduling delays at the reported access site, and they verified that a patched build eliminated it under the same load. Weighing the version boundary, the host dispersion, and the sanitizer output, the postmortem review board concluded that the corruption originated in a race condition in the replication code. That conclusion settles the tension between the faulty module and the sanitizer finding: the cell was a genuine defect warranting replacement, but it was not the source of this corruption.\n\nThe final stage of the postmortem concerns impact rather than cause, and it demands the same rigor. Engineers reconcile replicas against backups and write-ahead logs to establish whether any acknowledged write was irrecoverably altered. Either no customer data was permanently lost in the incident, or some customer data was permanently lost in the incident; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Determining which outcome obtained governs customer notification, regulatory reporting, and the scope of remediation, so it is documented with the same evidentiary standard applied to root cause.",
